module gunbc.memory_stall_refusal

import std.types { Int, String, NonEmptyStr, Bool }
import std.disposition { Disposition, Terminal }
import extdeps.linux.procfs { ProcSelfStat, procfs_path_literal }

data memory_stall_refusal_authority_disposition: Disposition = Terminal {
  reason: "Single authority for the STALL half of the memory governor: the decision that a resolve which is thrashing -- spending its wall time refaulting its own evicted pages rather than computing -- must stop the line with a typed refusal instead of holding indefinitely. The budget half (what bound to admit against) is gunbc.host_budget_source; the capacity half (how many typed modules the cache may hold) is gunbc.typed_module_cache_capacity; this module owns what happens when both of those were satisfied and the run still cannot make progress, which is the state neither of them can see: the budget was readable and admitted, the cap was derived and enforced, and the machine underneath cannot actually deliver the budget the operator declared."
}

data memory_stall_observed_receipt_note: String = "THE MEASURED STALL THIS MODULE REFUSES, so the thresholds below face a real specimen rather than a thought experiment. 2026-08-30, default BuildBuddy remote-execution VM (7276 MiB total, 6 CPUs, swap 0, no procfs PSI, no cgroup memory controller files at any depth), GUNBC_MEMORY_BUDGET_BYTES=7000000000: four whole-closure gunbc runs (invocations 0cf5e9cb-f09e-47b6-a953-9bb4528ea9ec, e11bc9bf-61a8-432f-b596-e1d74efc5b55, ecd54e06-8b02-48c6-9f63-95e912c9edff, 33d93219-793c-40fe-a2c1-84f64d0fb743, measured by session loyal-raven-297) ran 2 to 4.5 hours without completing and without printing a further line, while the same census completes in about 10 minutes on a 20 GB runner. The budget was READABLE (env override) and ADMITTED; the machine simply cannot hold the working set beside its own page cache, and with swap disabled the kernel's only recourse is evicting file-backed pages -- including the binary's own text -- which the process immediately refaults. Wall time is consumed by disk reads the process never asked for; memory.current never crosses a kill line; no signal any existing arm reads ever fires. The run is not slow, it is a treadmill.\n\nWHY NO EXISTING ARM COVERS IT. gunbc.host_budget_source refuses an UNREADABLE budget -- this budget was readable. gunbc.whole_corpus_compile_admission refuses a whole-root subject below measured demand -- these were Entry subjects, deliberately outside that arm (whole_corpus_compile_scope_note reserves the gap). gunbc.typed_module_cache_capacity bounds the typed cache -- the thrash is at PAGE grain underneath the allocator, invisible to entry counts. And the deleted AIMD controller's PSI arm could not run here even if it survived: the VM synthesizes no /proc/pressure files at all (measured, same day)."

data memory_stall_signal_choice_note: String = "WHY THE SIGNAL IS THE PROCESS'S OWN MAJOR-FAULT COUNTER AND NOTHING HOST-SHARED. majflt in /proc/self/stat (proc.5 field 12) counts the times THIS process had to read one of its own pages back from disk: it is the kernel's record of eviction-and-readmission at the grain the kernel actually evicts, observed about this process alone. A warm, healthy process sustains approximately zero major faults after its inputs are first read, whatever its size and however slow its arithmetic -- so a slow-but-progressing resolve and a thrashing one, which no wall-clock bound can tell apart, are cleanly separated by this counter: the first accumulates wall with majflt flat, the second accumulates majflt in the thousands per second. That is also why the verdict below carries NO elapsed-time arm: time appears only as the denominator of a rate, never as a refusal condition, because a timeout labelled pressure-detection would refuse exactly the honest slow case this signal exists to admit.\n\nWHAT WAS REJECTED. /proc/meminfo MemAvailable is a fact about the MACHINE and is precisely the absorbing substitution gunbc.host_budget_source deleted (host_budget_unreadable_cgroup_receipt_note); reusing it here as a trigger would re-open that arm one layer up. PSI (/proc/pressure/memory) is the kernel's own stall accounting and would be preferable where it exists, but the VM this class was measured on does not synthesize it (no /proc/pressure, no cgroup controllers -- measured 2026-08-30), so an arm keyed on PSI is permanently green exactly where the harm lives. The typed-module cache's eviction/readmission counters are the governor's own bookkeeping and ARE carried in the refusal so the operator can see whether the cache was implicated, but they do not gate: recompute-on-miss across entries is designed-in cost, and no measured stall has yet implicated it (the observed specimens thrash below the allocator, at page grain). If a measured stall ever shows the cache readmitting the same module unboundedly, the threshold for that axis is added HERE with its receipt, not improvised at a call site."

data memory_stall_admitted_under_pressure_receipt_note: String = "THE MEASURED CONTROL THAT FORCED THE CPU CONJUNCT, found by this refusal's own first execution rather than by reasoning. On the first CI run carrying a rate-only form of this verdict (gunbc PR #9755, run 33319823294, required-witnesses-floor, srv3 actions-runner slice, budget 15 GiB from cgroup memory.high), the arm fired at 12431 major faults/minute sustained over a 788-second window -- one 13-minute typecheck of std.constructors under the slice's memory.high reclaim throttle. That configuration is not a treadmill: the same floor completes green on main on the same runners day after day. A resolve can sustain hundreds of refaults per second under a reclaim throttle and still be spending most of its wall COMPUTING -- which is exactly the slow-but-progressing state this module is forbidden to refuse. Rate alone cannot separate the two; what separates them is where the wall goes. A progressing run under pressure still burns CPU between faults; the treadmill's defining property is that its CPU share of wall is near zero, because the process sits in disk wait re-reading its own text. So the refusal is a CONJUNCTION: the fault rate above its line AND the process's own CPU share of the window below its floor. Both halves are the process observing itself; neither is host-shared; neither is a timeout."

// POLICY THRESHOLDS, like TCP's and like the deleted AIMD controller's: dimensionless or
// budget-scaled, never a per-corpus measurement. The rate line sits two orders of
// magnitude above healthy background (a warm resolve sustains ~0 majflt/min) and well
// below both measured pressured specimens (12431/min progressing on the CI runner;
// the treadmill higher still). It is deliberately not asked to separate progressing from
// thrashing on its own -- the CPU-share conjunct does that (see the receipt note above).
// The window is what makes the rate a RATE rather than a spike detector: a verdict is
// only computable over at least this much wall, so one cold-start burst of refaults can
// never refuse a run that then progresses.
data memory_stall_major_fault_rate_per_minute_threshold: Int = 6000

data memory_stall_verdict_window_minimum_wall_ms: Int = 30000

// The progress floor of the conjunction: a window is a treadmill only if the process's
// own CPU (utime+stime) accounts for less than this percentage of the window's wall.
// The measured CI progressing-under-pressure specimen runs its 13-minute typecheck
// CPU-bound between faults; the measured treadmill's CPU share is near zero because the
// wall is disk wait. Twenty percent sits far above the treadmill and far below any
// computing phase, and a phase that genuinely computes for less than a fifth of its wall
// while refaulting past the rate line has no measured specimen -- if one appears, it is
// a receipt to file here, not a reason to widen silently.
data memory_stall_progress_cpu_share_percent_floor: Int = 20

type MemoryStallObservation = MemoryStallObservation {
  window_wall_ms: Int,
  major_faults_in_window: Int,
  self_cpu_ms_in_window: Int,
  cache_evictions_in_window: Int,
  cache_readmissions_in_window: Int,
}

// Three states, none conflated: a window too short to support a rate is OPEN (keep
// accumulating -- it is not evidence of progress, and it is not a refusal), a computable
// window below the line is PROGRESSING (however slow: majflt flat means the wall is being
// spent computing), and a computable window above the line is the refusal. The refusal
// carries the whole observation so the diagnostic can name what was seen rather than
// summarize it.
type MemoryStallVerdict
  = StallWindowOpen { window_wall_ms: Int, minimum_wall_ms: Int }
  | ProgressUnderMemoryAdmissible { major_faults_per_minute: Int, self_cpu_share_percent: Int }
  | StallRefusedPageThrash {
      major_faults_per_minute: Int,
      threshold_per_minute: Int,
      self_cpu_share_percent: Int,
      cpu_share_percent_floor: Int,
      observation: MemoryStallObservation,
    }

fn memory_stall_major_faults_per_minute(o: MemoryStallObservation) -> Int {
  match o {
    MemoryStallObservation {
      window_wall_ms: wall,
      major_faults_in_window: faults,
      self_cpu_ms_in_window: _,
      cache_evictions_in_window: _,
      cache_readmissions_in_window: _,
    } => faults * 60000 / wall
  }
}

fn memory_stall_self_cpu_share_percent(o: MemoryStallObservation) -> Int {
  match o {
    MemoryStallObservation {
      window_wall_ms: wall,
      major_faults_in_window: _,
      self_cpu_ms_in_window: cpu,
      cache_evictions_in_window: _,
      cache_readmissions_in_window: _,
    } => cpu * 100 / wall
  }
}

fn memory_stall_verdict(o: MemoryStallObservation) -> MemoryStallVerdict {
  match o {
    MemoryStallObservation {
      window_wall_ms: wall,
      major_faults_in_window: _,
      self_cpu_ms_in_window: _,
      cache_evictions_in_window: _,
      cache_readmissions_in_window: _,
    } =>
      if wall < memory_stall_verdict_window_minimum_wall_ms {
        StallWindowOpen {
          window_wall_ms: wall,
          minimum_wall_ms: memory_stall_verdict_window_minimum_wall_ms,
        }
      } else {
        if memory_stall_major_faults_per_minute(o: o) > memory_stall_major_fault_rate_per_minute_threshold
          && memory_stall_self_cpu_share_percent(o: o) < memory_stall_progress_cpu_share_percent_floor {
          StallRefusedPageThrash {
            major_faults_per_minute: memory_stall_major_faults_per_minute(o: o),
            threshold_per_minute: memory_stall_major_fault_rate_per_minute_threshold,
            self_cpu_share_percent: memory_stall_self_cpu_share_percent(o: o),
            cpu_share_percent_floor: memory_stall_progress_cpu_share_percent_floor,
            observation: o,
          }
        } else {
          ProgressUnderMemoryAdmissible {
            major_faults_per_minute: memory_stall_major_faults_per_minute(o: o),
            self_cpu_share_percent: memory_stall_self_cpu_share_percent(o: o),
          }
        }
      }
  }
}

fn memory_stall_verdict_refuses(v: MemoryStallVerdict) -> Bool {
  match v {
    StallWindowOpen { window_wall_ms: _, minimum_wall_ms: _ } => false
    ProgressUnderMemoryAdmissible { major_faults_per_minute: _, self_cpu_share_percent: _ } => false
    StallRefusedPageThrash {
      major_faults_per_minute: _,
      threshold_per_minute: _,
      self_cpu_share_percent: _,
      cpu_share_percent_floor: _,
      observation: _,
    } => true
  }
}

// The pressure clause of the refusal diagnostic: what was observed, against what line,
// from which counter, and the remedy. The LOCATION half (which entry, which module, which
// budget and source the run was admitted under) is composed by the consumer that holds
// those facts; this fn owns only what this module can know, so the two halves cannot
// disagree about the observation.
fn memory_stall_refusal_pressure_text(v: MemoryStallVerdict) -> String {
  match v {
    StallWindowOpen { window_wall_ms: _, minimum_wall_ms: _ } => ""
    ProgressUnderMemoryAdmissible { major_faults_per_minute: _, self_cpu_share_percent: _ } => ""
    StallRefusedPageThrash {
      major_faults_per_minute: rate,
      threshold_per_minute: line,
      self_cpu_share_percent: cpu_share,
      cpu_share_percent_floor: cpu_floor,
      observation: o,
    } =>
      match o {
        MemoryStallObservation {
          window_wall_ms: wall,
          major_faults_in_window: faults,
          self_cpu_ms_in_window: _,
          cache_evictions_in_window: evictions,
          cache_readmissions_in_window: readmissions,
        } =>
          join([
            "MemoryStallRefusedPageThrash: this process refaulted its own evicted pages at ",
            to_string(rate),
            " major faults/minute while computing for only ",
            to_string(cpu_share),
            "% of the wall, over the last ",
            to_string(wall),
            " ms (",
            to_string(faults),
            " major faults; rate line ",
            to_string(line),
            "/minute, CPU-share floor ",
            to_string(cpu_floor),
            "%; source ",
            procfs_path_literal(p: ProcSelfStat) as String,
            " majflt and utime+stime; typed-cache evictions in window ",
            to_string(evictions),
            ", readmissions ",
            to_string(readmissions),
            "). The wall clock is being spent re-reading resident pages the kernel evicted, not computing: the machine underneath cannot deliver the admitted budget beside its own page cache, and with swap disabled this treadmill holds indefinitely instead of failing. Refusing rather than holding. Remedy: run where the admitted budget is genuinely available (a larger runner), or scope the compile with --entry to a smaller closure.",
          ], "")
      }
  }
}
